1,cs3 $ Spores echinulate; saprobes growing on conifer needles or leaves, rarely on thin branches (fruitbodies often rather tough and thin-fleshed with ±brownish or greenish colours). $ Subgen. Echinoramaria, page 10 $ s"Subgen. Echinoramaria"
1,2 $ Spores smooth, striate or verrucose; saprobes or mycorrhizal
2,cs2 $ Rhizomorphs dimitic or fruitbodies growing on wood; saprobes; fruitbodies often rather tough and thin-fleshed. $ Subgen. Lentoramaria, page 8 $s" Subgen. Lentoramaria"
2,cs1 $ Rhizomorphs, if present, monomitic; mycorrhizal or rarely saprobes, growing on needles or leaves; fruitbodies often soft and ±fleshy. $ Subgen. Ramaria, page 2$s" Subgen. Ramaria"

rem $ Key to the species of Ramaria subgen. Ramaria
cs1,0$
1,2 $ Spore ornament of helical stripes. (The R. botrytis group)
1,3 $ Spore ornament of isolated to ±fused warts or rarely absent
2,0 $ Branches pinkish-buff (5A3), towards the tops pale flesh pink (7A2); tops ±red (about 9-10C6-7). Fruitbodies to 20x20 cm; clamps present, spores 12-15-18 ??4.5-5.3-6.0 µm,  with helical stripes, Q = 2.8-3.1. $ R. botrytis (Pers.: Fr.) Rick.
2,0 $ Branches cream, warm buff, pinkish buff to salmon (4-5A3-4,6A2-3), tops pale chrome, lemon chrome, buff yellow to orange yellow (3A3-8, 4A5-7). Fruitbodies to 20 ??20 cm; clamps present, spores 10.7-12.5-15.6 ??3.8-4.5-4.9 µm, smooth or with faint helical stribes, Q = 2.4-3.1. $ R. obtusissima var. incarnata CornerKEY TO THE SPECIES OF RAMARIA KNOWN FROM FENNOSCANDIA
3,4 $ Stem coral to brownish red (9B8, 9E7-8) in 10-20% KOH; branches of fresh, mature fruitbodies generally ±brown or violet to lilac (the R. fennica group)
3,7 $ Stem unchangeable or slightly darker in 10-20% KOH; branches pale (whitish, cream to pinkish buff) or intensely coloured (±yellow, orange, salmon or pink)
4,0 $ Stem and rhizomorphs with numerous, rod-shaped, 10-40 µm long crystals; surface and flesh quickly turning vinaceous when bruised; taste distinctly bitter. Fruitbodies to 11 ??8 cm; clamps present, spores 10.5-12.6-15.0 ??4.1-5.0-5.7 µ––m, Q = 2.3-2.6. $ R. testaceoflava (Bres.) Rick.
4,5 $  Stem with both small irregular and double-pyramidal crystals, rhizomorphs with small irregular crystals; not quickly turning vinaceous when bruised; taste mild to spicy, but probably never bitter 5
5,0 $ Clamps absent; 10% H2SO4 on stem negative or slightly more brown; spores with an ornament of rows or lines of isolated to fused warts or ridges; hyphae in base and especially in the rhizomorphs with encrustations of small, irregular crystals. Fruitbodies to 12 ??7 cm; spores (8.0-) 9.0- 11.5-13.0 ??5.0-5.6-6.2 µm, Q = 1.8-2.2. $ R. spinulosa (Pers.: Fr.) Quél.
5,6 $ Clamps present; 10% H2SO4 on stem pale orange (5A6) to flame scarlet (7B8); spores with an ornament of ±isolated warts; double-pyramidal and irregular crystals present in the base, small, irregular crystals numerous in the rhizomorphs
6,0 $ Tops either violet to lilac or light vinaceous grey (5D4-9D3); branches with rather grey-brown colours, often with clear violet to lilac coloured areas at the axils. Fruitbodies to 12 ?7 cm; clamps present, spores 9.0-11.3-13.0 ??4.5-5.1-5.7 µm, Q = 2.0-2.3. $ R. fennica (P.Karst.) Rick.
6,0 $ Tops for a long time cream to pinkish buff (4-5A3); branches yellowbrown to orangebrown, never with violet colours. Fruitbodies to 11 ??9 cm; clamps present, spores 9.0-9.9-11.1 ??3.7-4.3-4.9 µm, Q = 2.2-2.3. $ R. fascicaulis nom. prov.
7,8 $ Mature fruitbodies uniformly whitish, cream to pinkish buff
7,11 $ Mature fruitbodies at least locally ±yellow, orange, salmon to pink
8,0 $ Stem less then 1 cm thick; fruitbodies small, op to 6 (-8) ??4(-5) cm; clamps present; spore average length < 10 µm; probably a saprobe, growing on needles of Juniperus, conifers or under Salix in the subarctic/subalpine zones. Spores 8.2-9.3-10.5 ??3.3-4.1-5.0 µm, Q = 2.1-2.5. $ R. suecica (Fr.: Fr.) Donk
8,9 $ Stem mostly 1-5 cm thick; fruitbodies normally larger; clamps present or absent; spore Lm>11 µm; probably mycorrhizal, growing on soil in woods.
9,0 $ Clamps present; spores 12-15.3-18 (-20) ??4.8-6-7.5 µm, Q=2.3-2.7, older specimens often slowly turning fawn to vinaceous brown (about 7C4 to 10E4-5) when touched or bruised. Fruitbodies to 12 ??9 cm; spores Q = 2.3-2.7. $ R. megaspora nom. prov.
9,0 $ Clamps absent; spores smaller; unchangeable when bruised but often with pale violet spots around soil-particles, etc. 10 10. Fruitbodies ±cream to pinkish buff; smell strong, spicy; taste somewhat bitter; hyphae of basal tomentum or in base often with gelatinous coating. Fruitbodies to 25 ??20 cm; spores 10.5-12.5-13.5 ??4.9-5.7-6.6 µm, Q = 1.9-2.3. $ R. pallida (Schaeff.) Rick.
9,0 $ Fruitbodies whitish to pale pinkish buff; smell probably weak; taste mild or slightly bitter; hyphae of basal tomentum or in base probably without gelatinous coating. Fruitbodies to 22 ??12 cm; spores 9.0-12.2-13.0 ??4.5-5.2-6.0 µm, Q = 2.0-2.4. $ R. paludosa (Lundell) Schild  (possibly conspecific with R. pallida)
11,12 $ All parts ±clear yellow (straw yellow, pale yellow, lemon yellow, light chrome to lemon chrome (2-4A3-8) or dark pinkish buff (5B3-4)); branches often slowly darkening when bruised (possibly over night). (The R. flava group)
11,18 $ Branches or tops more orange to pink (pinkish buff, peach, saffron, apricot-orange, orange to ochraceous (5-6AB2-8,7A4-5)); branches generally unchangeable when bruised
12,0 $ Tops with dense, subcristate branching, more clear yellow than the branches; spores smooth. $ see R. schildii p. 6
12,13 $  Tops not subcristate, not more clear yellow than the branches; spores ornamented  13
13,0 $ Spores 5-7.5 µm broad; mature specimens dark pinkish buff (5B3-4), older specimens often slowly turning fawn to dark vinaceous (about 7C4 to 10E4-5) when bruised. $ see R. megaspora p. 4
13,14 $ Spores narrower; mature specimens ±ochraceous (5B4-5) to ±yellow, unchangeable or turning brownish to vinaceous when bruised
14,0 $ With brownish red (10DE7-8) spots at the base. Fruitbodies to 15 ??12 cm; clamps absent, spores (7.0-) 8.0-10.5-12.0 (-13.1) ??(3.5-) 4.0-4.5-5.5 µm, Q = 2.0-2.4. $ R. sanguinea (Pers.) Quél.
14,15 $ Never with reddish discolorations
15,0 $ Clamps present; branches turning dark vinaceous when bruised. Spores 10-13.2-16.5 ??3.7-4.6-6.0 µm, Q = 2.8-2.9. $ R. flava (Schaeff.) Quél.
15,16 $ Clamps absent; branches unchangeable
16,0 $ Spore Q = 2.3; branches and tops uniformly pale chrome, cream to warm buff (3-4A3-4); stem often with abortive branches. Fruitbodies to 12 ??11 cm; spores 9.4-10.2-11.0 ?4.1-4.4-4.9 µm. $ R. lutea (Vitt.) Schild
16,17 $ Spore Q = 2.5-3.5; branches of mature specimens warm buff, dark pinkish buff to ochraceous (4A4, 5B4-5 (-6B4)) sometimes remaining ±pale cream ((3-)4A2) at unexposed areas of the upper stem/lower branches, tops pale cream (4A2); stem without abortive branches
17,0 $ Spores 9.0-15 ??3.7-6.0 µm, Q = 2.5-2.9; branches warm buff (4A4) or only slightly pinkish-buff (5A4 (6A3)). $ R. corneri var. corneri nom. prov. (syn.: R. subtilis ss. auct.)
17,0 $ Spores 12.3-16.0-18.0 (-20) ??4.1-4.7-5.5 µm, Q = 3.1-3.5; branches of mature specimens dark pinkish buff to ochraceous with a shade of clay pink (5B4-5 (-6B4)). Fruitbodies to 14 ??14 cm. $ R. corneri var. cylindrospora nom. prov.
18,19 $ Tops of mature fruitbodies paler and more clear yellow than the branches (pale chrome to orange yellow – 3-4A2-7 (5A5-6)); branches often pinkish buff, salmon, flesh-pink to pale reddish orange (5A2-4, 6A3-5, 7A4-5), never(?) with a clear yellow colour band below; clamps present. (The R. formosa group)
18,23 $ Tops of mature specimens concolorous or more orange than the branches; branches of mature specimens saffron, apricot-orange, peach, orange to ochraceous (5-6AB5-8), often with a clear yellow colour band in the transition-zone between the pale stem and the coloured branches, especially in areas less exposed to light; clamps present or absent. (The R. aurea group)
19,20 $ Spores smooth
19,21 $ Spores ornamented (check in cotton blue)
20,0 $ Tops rounded to swollen see R. obtusissima var incarnata, p. 2  Tops with dense, subcristate branching. Fruitbodies to 12 ?7 cm; branched 6-8 times, base/mainbranches and smaller branches sinuate with the tops pointing in various directions; branches pale pinkish buff to pinkish buff (5A2-3); tops pale chrome to cream (3/4A3); clamps present, spores 12-13.8-164-4.4-5.2 (-5.6) µm, smooth. $ R. schildii R.H. Petersen
21,0 $ Stem large, up to 6 ??6 cm; young fruitbodies with yellowish branches; mature fruitbodies with pale pinkish buff to pinkish buff (5A2-4, 5B4) branches. Fruitbodies to 18 ??18 cm; clamps present, spores 9.8-12.0-13.5 ??4.1-5.1-6.2 µm, Q = 2.2-2.5. $ R. flavescens (Schaeff.) R.H.Peters.
21,22 $ Stem small, typically less then 2 ??2 cm or fruitbodies branching from the base; young fruitbodies with ±pink to reddish branches; mature fruitbodies with pinkish buff to salmon (5A4, 6A3-4) branches
22,0 $ Fruitbodies to 15 cm high; clamps present; flesh green in FeSO4, chalky-friable when dry. Fruitbodies often dividing from the base into several main branches; spores 9.8-12.1-15.0 ??4.9-5.5-6.5 µm, Q = 2.1-2.4. $ R. formosa (Pers.: Fr.) Quél.
22,0 $ Fruitbodies rather small, up to 7 (-10) cm high; clamps absent; flesh not green in FeSO4, not chalky-friable when dry. Fruitbodies with a small, up to 2.5 cm thick stem or branched from the base. Spores 7-13 (-16) ??3.0-4.5 (-5.7) µm; Q = 2.6-3.0. $ R. fagetorum Schild ss.auct., non ss. Mass Geesteranus
23,24 $ Clamps absent
24,25 $ Clamps present, at least at the base of the basidia
24,0 $ Fruitbodies rather small, up to 7 (-10) cm high, with a small, up to 2.5 cm thick stem $ see R. fagetorum, p 6
24,0 $ Fruitbodies medium-sized with a prominent, 2.5-6 cm thick stem; branches and tops yellow-orange to salmon, long with a clear yellow colour band at the upper stem/lower branches; spore Q = 2.3-2.6. Spores 10.0-13.0 ??4.0-6.0 µm. $ R. aurea (Schaeff.) Quél.
25,0 $ Spores smooth to almost smooth. Fruitbodies to 14 ??11 cm; spores 10.0-11.1-13.0 ??3.5-4.0-4.5 µm, Q = 2.8. $ R. magnipes Marr & Stuntz
25,26 $ Spores distinctly warted
26,0 $ Spore Q = 2.7-3.0; clamps present at the base of the basidia, but not always at the other hyphae. Fruitbodies to 16 ??17 cm; upper branches pinkish buff, saffron, salmon to peach(5-6A3-6); tops apricot orange to orange (5-6A8); spores 11.0-13.9-15.6 ??3.5-4.8-5.7 µm, with distinct, up to 0,4 µm high, slightly fused warts and sinuous ridges. $ R. largentii Marr & Stuntz
26,27 $ Spore Q = 2.3-2.6; clamps present at all septae
27,0 $ Tops at mature specimens apricot orange to ochraceous (5AB7-8). Branches pinkish-puff, saffron to salmon (5A3-5,6A3), tops apricot orange to ochraceous (5AB7-8); clamps present at all septa. Fruitbodies to 20 ??28 cm; spores 10.0-11.6-13.0 ??4.0-4.4-5.0 µm, Q = 2.3-2.6. $ R. flavicingula R.H. Petersen
27,0 $ Tops at mature specimens cream to warm buff (4A3). $ see R. flavescens, p. 6
**********************



