rem $ Key to the species of Ramaria subgen. Lentoramaria
cs2,0$
1,0 $ Spores smooth; with a prominent, white basal tomentum;  stem and branches of mature specimens pale yellowish. Fruitbodies to 6 ??4 cm; spores 7-11 ??3-4 µm. $ Lentaria dendroidea (Fr.) J.H. Petersen
1,2 $ Spores ornamented with rounded warts; without prominent tomentum (but possibly with white, adpressed hyphae); colours of mature specimens often different
2,0 $ With a pronounced smell of aniseed; fruitbodies uniformly whitish, pinkish buff to pale salmon; without colour change when bruised; all parts dimitic; on conifer needles. Fruitbodies to 9 ??3 cm; spores 5.5-6.3-7 ??3.3-3.8-4.5 µm; Q = 1.5-1.8. $ R. gracilis (Pers.: Fr.) Quél.
2,3 $ Smell weak, spicy or spicy-sweet; fruitbodies yellowish, ±brown or green; often darker when bruised; stem and branches monomitic; on wood or at the base of grasses
3,4 $ Fruitbody or flesh with ±green areas; on conifer wood
3,5 $ Fruitbody without green; on conifer wood, deciduous wood or at the base of grasses
4,0 $ Rhizomorphs monomitic, spores 8.2-12.3 ??4.1-5.5 µm, rough in outline, ornament cyanophilous. Fruitbodies to 10 ??10 cm; Q = 2.1-2.2. $ R. apiculata (Fr.: Fr.) Donk
4,0 $ Rhizomorphs dimitic, spores 7.8-10.7 ??4.1-4.9 µm, finely rough to almost smooth in outline, ornament weakly cyanophilous to acyanophilous. Fruitbodies to 5 ??3 cm; Q =2.0-2.1. $ R. tsugina (Peck) Marr & Stuntz
5,0 $ Spores 6.5-9 ??4-5.5 µm, Q = 1.6-1.8; rhizomorphs purple in 10% KOH; branches “salmon“ to “red brown“. Fruitbodies to 10 ??6 cm. $ R. rubella (Schaeff.) R.H. Petersen
5,6 $ Spores longer, Q = 1.9-2.2; rhizomorphs unchangeable or slightly brown in 10% KOH; branches yellowish, pinkish buff to greyish brown
6,0 $ On deciduous wood; young fruitbodies pale yellow (see cover picture); mature fruitbodies pinkish buff, clay pink, greyish brown to fawn (5AB3, 6BD3-4, 7E5); tops yellow. Fruitbodies to 11 ??8 cm; spores 7.5-9-10.5 ??4-4.4-5 µm; Q= 1.9-2.2. $ R. stricta (Pers.: Fr.) Quél.
6,7 $ On conifer wood or at the base of Ammophila arenaria;young fruitbodies whitish to pinkish buff; mature fruitbodies pale pinkish-buff, clay buff to cinnamon (5A2-3,6CD5-6); whitish towards the tops, but extreme tops sometimes abruptly pale chrome (3A3-4)
7,0 $ On Ammophila arenaria; fruitbodies in all ages pale pinkish buff (5A2-3); extreme tops often pale chrome. Fruitbodies to 11 ??7 cm; spores 8.2-9.1-10.3 ??4-4.4-5 µm; Q = 2.0-2.2. $ R. ammophila nom. prov.
7,0 $ On conifer wood; mature fruitbodies clay buff to cinnamon (6CD5-6); extreme tops whitish or concolourous with the branches. Fruitbodies to 10 ??7 cm; spores as R. ammophila. $ R. concolor R.H. Petersen
****************
rem $ Key to the species of Ramaria subgen. Echinoramaria
(Note: All known taxa from the area (except the dubious R. argentea) are keyed out. Some of these may later prove synonymous).
cs3,0$
1,0 $ Spores 13-20 × 5-8.2 µm, with up to 1.5 (-2) µm long spines; all basidia two-spored; all parts quickly turning pink to deep brown when bruised (sect. Dendrocladium). Fruitbodies to 8 × 4 cm. $ R. broomei (Cotton & Wakef.) R.H. Petersen
1,2 $  Spores and spines smaller; basidia mostly four-spored; fruitbodies not brownish on bruising (sect. Flaccidae) 2
2,3 $ With green colours or bruising green
2,4 $ Without greenish colours
3,0 $ Spores 6.5-10.5 × 3.5-4.9 µm, Q = 2.0-2.4; fruitbodies to 6 cm high and 4 cm broad, generally rather delicate with an up to 0.5 cm thick stem. Spore ornament to 0.5 µm high. $ R. abietina (Pers.: Fr.) Quél.
3,0 $ Spores 10.7-13.7 × 4.5-6 µm, Q = 2.1-2.5; fruitbodies to 13 cm high and 7.5 cm broad, generally rather stout with an up to 2.5 cm thick stem. Spore ornament to 0.5 µm high. $ R. echinovirens Corner, Thind & Dev.
4,5 $ Rhizomorphs with irregular crystals or crystals absent
4,8 $ Rhizomorphs with star-shaped crystals
5,0 $ Tops pale lilac (16-17A2-3), flattened-subcristate; spores 6-9 × 3-3.7 µm; Q = 2.0-2.4; spore ornament to 0.5 µm high. Fruitbodies to 3.5 × 3 cm. $ R. subdecurrens (Coker) Corner
5,6 $ Without lavender colours
6,0 $ Spores 7-12 × 3.3-5 µm, Q = 2.0-2.1; branches buff yellow, pinkish buff, ochraceous to yellowish brown; hymenium amphigenous; taste mild. Fruitbodies to 8 × 5 cm; spore ornament to 1.2 µm high. $ R. eumorpha (P. Karst.) Corner
6,7 $  Spores 5-9 µm long, Q = d 1.9; branches sometimes darker; hymenium unilateral; taste bitter or pepperish. 7
7,0 $ Branches fuscous to deep olive; spores 5.2-8.9 × 3.0-4.4 µm, Q = 1.7-1.9. Fruitbodies to 16 × 10 cm; spore ornament to 1µm high. $ R. mutabilis Schild & R.H.Petersen
7,0 $ Branches ochraceous to cinnamon, at age fawn; spores 4.8-7.2 × 2.8-4.0 µm, Q = 1.5-1.8. Fruitbodies to 10 × 5 cm; spore ornament to 1 µm high. $ R. luteolus nom. prov.
8,9 $ On needles of conifers
8,10 $ In deciduous forest or on the ground with mosses and lichens
9,0 $ Spores 3.2-6.4 × 2.5-4 µm, Q = 1.5-1.9, spore ornament to 0.3 µm high. Fruitbodies to 4 × 3 cm. $ R. corrugata (P.Karst) Schild (syn.: R. myceliosa)
9,0 $ Spores 7.0-9.5 × 3.6-4.9 µm, Q = 1.8-2.1, spore ornament to 0.5 (-0.8) µm high. Fruitbodies to 5 × 3 cm. $ R. flaccida (Fr.) Bourd.
10,0 $ On the ground with mosses and lichens. fruitbodies up to 3,5 cm high and 2,4 cm broad but often smaller; spores 6.5-8.0 × 3.5-4.5 µm, Q = 1.7-2.0. $ R. roellinii Schild
10,11$ In deciduous forest
11,0 $ Stem > 0.5 cm thick, fruitbodies up to 14 cm high; spores 5.2-6.7 × 3.0-4.1 µm, Q = 1.5-1.7. $ R. decurrens (Pers.) R.H.Petersen
11,0 $ Stem < 0.5 cm thick, fruitbodies less than 4 (-6) cm high; spores 6.5-9.0 × 2.9-5.0 µm, Q = 1.8-2.1. $ R. elegans nom. prov.








